{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#2659","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#2659","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#2659\",\"parent_revision\":\"82ed78e6eb2ebb42d4453f3b3a62c9ca056a97b92168d4a0a58459b9d7005364\",\"root_revision\":\"82ed78e6eb2ebb42d4453f3b3a62c9ca056a97b92168d4a0a58459b9d7005364\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P2\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-12\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThe backlog MCP server has no tool to delete or remove an artifact registration. When an agent registers an artifact incorrectly (wrong artifact_id, wrong type, premature registration), the only remediation available is marking it archived or superseded via `artifact_register` upsert.\\n\\nThis leaves stale entries that future agents must interpret and reason around. It also means a deviation from protocol cannot be cleanly undone \u2014 the entry persists and requires orchestrator-level reconciliation to determine which registration is authoritative.\\n\\nObserved today: an agent on the incremental sam_plan path manually registered a task-plan artifact it should not have. With no delete tool available, it archived the registration and made the deviation loud in its STATUS report so the orchestrator could reconcile. This is fragile and relies on the orchestrator correctly interpreting a non-standard artifact state.\\n\\nA delete or invalidate tool would allow clean recovery from erroneous registrations without leaving persistent stale state.\\n\\n## Groomed (2026-06-20)\\n\\n### Priority\\n\\n7/10 \u2014 The workaround (archive + STATUS escalation) works but is fragile, relies on orchestrator interpretation of non-standard state, and leaves permanent stale entries. The fix is low-risk and the business logic is already implemented. Not P1 because the workaround is available; ranked above mid because stale entries silently mislead future agents.\\n\\n### Impact\\n\\n- Blocks: Clean recovery from agent protocol deviations on the `sam_plan` incremental path and any workflow where `artifact_register` is called prematurely or with wrong parameters.\\n- Bottleneck: Every erroneous registration requires orchestrator-level reconciliation to determine which `artifact_list` entry is authoritative \u2014 this adds cognitive load and fragility to all artifact-consuming agents (`feature-researcher`, `t0-baseline-capture`, `tn-verification-gate`, `code-reviewer`, `complete-implementation`).\\n\\n### Benefits\\n\\n- Clean recovery from erroneous registrations without persistent stale state.\\n- Removes the need for orchestrators to reason about `archived` entries that represent mistakes rather than intentional lifecycle transitions.\\n- Makes `artifact_list` output trustworthy: every entry is either current, superseded by design, or explicitly deleted \u2014 not archived-because-wrong.\\n- Symmetry with `artifact_register`: agents can undo what they registered, making the manifest a correctable record.\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `artifact_delete(item_id, artifact_type, artifact_id)` is called:\\n\\n- If an entry matching `(artifact_type, artifact_id)` exists in the manifest for `item_id`: the entry is removed, the updated manifest is persisted via `provider.set_manifest()`, and the tool returns `deleted=True, action=\\\"removed\\\", artifact_count=<remaining count>`.\\n- If no matching entry exists: the manifest is unchanged, the tool returns `deleted=False, action=\\\"not_found\\\", artifact_count=<unchanged count>`. This is not an error \u2014 it is a no-op.\\n- Unrelated entries (different type or different artifact_id) are not affected.\\n- Subsequent `artifact_list(item_id)` calls do not show the deleted entry.\\n- The tool does not delete stored artifact content from the remote backend (Gist file, Linear attachment, GitLab snippet) in the initial implementation \u2014 it removes only the manifest entry. A warning is emitted to inform the caller that content may still exist remotely.\\n\\n### Desired Structure\\n\\nAn `artifact_delete` MCP tool endpoint in `backlog_core/server.py` that:\\n\\n- Accepts `item_id`, `artifact_type`, and `artifact_id` parameters following existing tool conventions.\\n- Delegates to `ArtifactRegistry.remove()` via the `get_manifest` / `set_manifest` round-trip already used by `artifact_register`.\\n- Returns a structured response (`deleted`, `action`, `artifact_count`, `messages`, `warnings`).\\n- Uses `ToolAnnotations(destructiveHint=True, idempotentHint=True)` \u2014 destructive because it mutates the manifest; idempotent because calling it twice produces the same final state.\\n- Emits a warning when the content storage layer (Gist/Snippet/Linear attachment) is not cleaned up.\\n- Is covered by at least one integration test verifying that `artifact_list` does not return the deleted entry after the call.\\n\\n### Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n1. Given `artifact_register(item_id=N, artifact_type=\\\"task-plan\\\", artifact_id=\\\"plan/wrong.yaml\\\")` followed by `artifact_delete(item_id=N, artifact_type=\\\"task-plan\\\", artifact_id=\\\"plan/wrong.yaml\\\")`, a subsequent `artifact_list(item_id=N)` returns no entry with `artifact_id=\\\"plan/wrong.yaml\\\"`.\\n2. Given a manifest with entries `[(\\\"feature-context\\\", \\\"plan/fc.md\\\"), (\\\"task-plan\\\", \\\"plan/tp.yaml\\\")]` and a call to `artifact_delete(item_id=N, artifact_type=\\\"task-plan\\\", artifact_id=\\\"plan/tp.yaml\\\")`, `artifact_list(item_id=N)` returns exactly one entry (`feature-context`) \u2014 the unrelated entry is untouched.\\n3. Given `artifact_delete(item_id=N, artifact_type=\\\"task-plan\\\", artifact_id=\\\"plan/does-not-exist.yaml\\\")` on a manifest that does not contain that entry, the tool returns `deleted=False, action=\\\"not_found\\\"` without an `error` key \u2014 not-found is not an error.\\n4. Given two sequential calls to `artifact_delete` with identical parameters, the second call returns `deleted=False, action=\\\"not_found\\\"` \u2014 the operation is idempotent.\\n5. Given the tool is called, the response contains `deleted` (bool), `action` (str), `artifact_count` (int), `messages` (list), and `warnings` (list) \u2014 consistent with the response shape of `artifact_register`.\\n6. Given content was stored for the artifact (via `artifact_register` with `content=`), calling `artifact_delete` emits a warning indicating that remote content storage was not cleaned up in this call.\\n\\n### Resources\\n\\n| Type | Item |\\n|------|------|\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_registry.py` \u2014 `ArtifactRegistry.remove()` method (line 272) |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/artifact_provider.py` \u2014 `ArtifactBackend` protocol, `get_manifest` / `set_manifest` methods |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` \u2014 `artifact_register` tool (line 3203) as implementation template |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/tests_backlog/test_artifact_registry.py` \u2014 existing unit tests for `remove()` covering existing-entry, missing-entry, exact-match-only cases |\\n| Prior work | `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_artifact_content_storage.py` \u2014 content storage integration tests |\\n| Docs | `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 Artifact Manifest System section (MCP tool list, type ownership table, backend behavior) |\\n\\n### Dependencies\\n\\n- Depends on: None \u2014 `ArtifactRegistry.remove()` and `ArtifactBackend.get_manifest/set_manifest` are already implemented and tested.\\n- Blocks: None identified. Content storage deletion (if required by the human answer above) would be a follow-on item.\\n\\n### Effort\\n\\nSmall \u2014 the business logic is already implemented and tested. The work is: one MCP tool function in `server.py` (following the `artifact_register` pattern), one integration test verifying `artifact_list` returns no deleted entry, and a warning for residual remote content. If content storage deletion is in scope, complexity increases to Medium due to the `ArtifactBackend` protocol extension and four backend implementations.\\n\\n### Artifact Classification\\n\\n- Primary artifact type: MCP tool\\n- Behavioral boundary: Tool call contract \u2014 `artifact_delete(item_id, artifact_type, artifact_id)` removes the manifest entry and returns a structured result indicating whether a deletion occurred.\\n- Domain-semantic evaluation target: Acceptance criteria must verify that calling the tool removes the entry from the live manifest, leaves unrelated entries untouched, handles the \\\"not found\\\" case without error, and does not produce stale entries that future `artifact_list` calls surface.\\n\\n### Blockers\\n\\n- Human decision required: scope of content storage deletion (manifest-entry-only vs full content purge from remote backend). Implementing full content purge requires extending `ArtifactBackend` protocol with a `delete_artifact_content` method and four backend implementations.\\n- Human decision required: caller-identity enforcement policy.\\n\\n### Output / Evidence\\n\\n- Session transcript referenced in the issue: agent on the incremental `sam_plan` path manually registered a `task-plan` artifact it should not have. The workaround was archiving the registration and making the deviation explicit in the STATUS report. The orchestrator had to reconcile which registration was authoritative.\\n- `backlog_core/artifact_registry.py` line 272: `remove()` method exists and is tested but is not wired to any MCP tool endpoint.\\n- `backlog_core/server.py`: `grep artifact_register|artifact_list|artifact_get|artifact_read` \u2014 four artifact tools exposed; no `artifact_delete`.\\n\\n### Questions for Human\\n\\n- Should `artifact_delete` also attempt to remove the stored content from the remote backend (Gist file, Linear attachment, GitLab snippet)? The `ArtifactBackend` protocol currently has no `delete_artifact_content` method. Implementing content deletion would require extending the protocol and all four backend implementations. The simpler initial scope removes only the manifest entry and warns about residual content. Confirm which scope is correct for this item.\\n- Should the tool enforce any caller-identity check \u2014 e.g., only the registering agent (matched via the `agent` field on `ArtifactEntry`) may delete the entry? Currently no such constraint exists anywhere in the artifact system. Confirm whether open-to-any-caller is acceptable.\\n\\n### RT-ICA Assessment\\n\\n- Goal: Expose `ArtifactRegistry.remove()` as an MCP tool so agents can cleanly delete erroneous artifact registrations instead of leaving stale entries that require orchestrator reconciliation.\\n- Status: APPROVED-WITH-GAPS\\n- AVAILABLE:\\n  - Business logic already exists: `ArtifactRegistry.remove(manifest, artifact_type, path)` in `backlog_core/artifact_registry.py` (line 272) filters by exact `(type, artifact_id)` match and returns a new manifest. Unit tests cover: existing entry removal, no-op on missing entry, exact-match-only semantics.\\n  - Full `ArtifactBackend` protocol already provides `get_manifest` + `set_manifest` round-trip used by all four backends (GitHub Gist, Linear, GitLab, LocalFilesystem).\\n  - All existing artifact MCP tools (`artifact_register`, `artifact_list`, `artifact_get`, `artifact_read`) follow the same `asyncio.to_thread(_run)` pattern in `backlog_core/server.py`, giving a clear implementation template.\\n  - `ToolAnnotations` pattern with `destructiveHint=True` is available and appropriate for this tool.\\n- DERIVABLE:\\n  - Tool input schema: `item_id` + `artifact_type` + `artifact_id` \u2014 derivable from existing tools' parameter conventions. No new model types needed.\\n  - Tool output schema: `deleted` (bool), `artifact_count` (int), `action` (`\\\"removed\\\"` or `\\\"not_found\\\"`), and `output`/`warnings` \u2014 derivable from `RegisterResult` model and existing tool return shapes.\\n  - `destructiveHint=True` annotation: derivable from `ToolAnnotations` usage in server.py \u2014 this is a mutation that removes an entry.\\n  - \\\"Not found\\\" is not an error: derivable from `ArtifactRegistry.remove()` no-op behavior \u2014 tool should return `deleted=False, action=\\\"not_found\\\"` without raising, consistent with how the business logic handles the missing-entry case.\\n- MISSING:\\n  - Content storage deletion: whether the tool should also delete stored artifact content from the Gist/Snippet/attachment (not just the manifest entry). The `ArtifactBackend` protocol has no `delete_artifact_content` method. The stored content blob may continue to exist on the remote backend even after the manifest entry is removed. This policy gap needs a decision.\\n  - Permission/audit model: whether non-owner agents should be allowed to delete registrations they did not create. No ownership field exists in `ArtifactEntry`; `agent` is a string but is not enforced as an access control gate.\\n\\n### Reproducibility\\n\\n1. Register an incorrect artifact: `artifact_register(item_id=N, artifact_type=\\\"task-plan\\\", artifact_id=\\\"plan/wrong-plan.yaml\\\")`\\n2. Confirm it is listed: `artifact_list(item_id=N)` \u2014 observe the erroneous entry in the response.\\n3. Attempt deletion: no `artifact_delete` tool exists \u2014 the only remediation is `artifact_register(item_id=N, artifact_type=\\\"task-plan\\\", artifact_id=\\\"plan/wrong-plan.yaml\\\", status=\\\"archived\\\")`.\\n4. Observe: `artifact_list(item_id=N)` still shows the entry with `status=archived`. Future agents calling `artifact_list` or `artifact_get` must filter or reason around this entry.\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **no artifact delete tool \u2014 incorrect registrations can only be archived not removed** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session transcript \u2014 swarm-task-planner agent working on plan Pe7547575 (2026-06-19)\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-06-19\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"017d9cfa8ab3e8b2680bc4edebd172ffb69c63480e46327e7bef034c8f4b1555\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABPTBnCA\"}"}